; SAVEBYTECODE: compile+run this program, then write its OWN compiled
; bytecode out to disk. Run this file first, THEN
; examples/bytecode_load.logo (in a separate `bin/logo` run) to load
; and re-run it standalone -- not even this .logo source is needed the
; second time. See docs/ROADMAP.md's "Bytecode save/load/assembler"
; section (Stages A-D) and docs/COMMAND_REFERENCE.md's "Files" entry.
;
; SAVEBYTECODE saves the WHOLE currently-running program -- every
; top-level statement in THIS file, not just the ones that already ran
; -- so it has to be the LAST statement here: a later statement in this
; same file would end up baked into the saved copy too, and (if that
; statement were itself a LOADBYTECODE of this very file) reloading it
; would recurse forever. examples/bytecode_load.logo is a genuinely
; separate script/compilation for exactly this reason.

TO SQUARE :SIDE
  REPEAT 4 [FD :SIDE RT 90]
END

PRINT "drawing-a-square
SQUARE 50
SAVEBYTECODE "scratch_bytecode.lgb
